## no port-forward
error: Cannot reach the controller at http://<server>. If it runs in a cluster, start a port-forward in another terminal first; for a default install: kubectl -n kube-neuron port-forward service/kubeneuron-controller 8080:8080
cause: Get "http://<server>/api/v1/version": dial tcp <server>: connect: connection refused

## no token
error: No API token was given. Read it from the installation's Secret; for a default install: export KUBENEURONCTL_TOKEN=$(kubectl -n kube-neuron get secret kubeneuron-operator-api-token -o jsonpath='{.data.token}' | base64 -d)
cause: 401 Unauthorized: operator authentication failed

## wrong token
error: The controller did not accept the API token. Read the current token from the installation's Secret; for a default install: export KUBENEURONCTL_TOKEN=$(kubectl -n kube-neuron get secret kubeneuron-operator-api-token -o jsonpath='{.data.token}' | base64 -d)
cause: 401 Unauthorized: operator authentication failed

## operator API disabled
error: The controller's operator API is off because the controller has no API token configured. Check the operator API token Secret of the installation and the controller's logs
cause: 503 Service Unavailable: operator API disabled: no API token configured

## token without permission
error: The controller recognised the credentials, but they do not allow this request. Use a token whose role allows this command
cause: 403 Forbidden: operator authentication failed

## too many failed sign-ins
error: The controller is refusing requests from this address after repeated failed sign-ins. Wait one minute, check the token, then try again
cause: 429 Too Many Requests: too many failed authentication attempts

## standby
error: This controller Pod is not the elected leader. Connect through the controller Service
cause: 503 Service Unavailable: standby: not the elected leader

## older controller
error: This controller does not support kubeneuronctl pools; it is from an older release. Upgrade the controller, or use the kubeneuronctl of the controller's release
cause: 404 Not Found: 404 page not found

## expired certificate
error: The controller's certificate has expired or is not valid yet, or this computer's clock is wrong. Check the clock and the certificate's validity dates; if the certificate has expired, renew it where HTTPS terminates
cause: Get "https://<server>/api/v1/version": tls: failed to verify certificate: x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid: current time <now> is after <not-after>

## certificate for another name
error: The controller's certificate is not valid for 127.0.0.1. Use an address the certificate lists in --server, or add this name to the certificate
cause: Get "https://<server>/api/v1/version": tls: failed to verify certificate: x509: cannot validate certificate for 127.0.0.1 because it doesn't contain any IP SANs

## certificate not for server use
error: The controller's certificate could not be verified. Check the certificate where HTTPS terminates
cause: Get "https://<server>/api/v1/version": tls: failed to verify certificate: x509: certificate specifies an incompatible key usage

## untrusted CA
error: The controller's certificate is signed by a CA this computer does not trust. Save the CA from the controller's TLS Secret and pass it with --ca-file: kubectl -n <namespace> get secret <secret> -o jsonpath='{.data.ca\.crt}' | base64 -d > kubeneuron-ca.crt
cause: Get "https://<server>/api/v1/version": tls: failed to verify certificate: <platform verifier text>

## https to a plain-HTTP controller
error: The controller answered over plain HTTP. Use an http:// address in --server only through a port-forward or another trusted connection: over plain HTTP the API token is sent unencrypted
cause: Get "https://<server>/api/v1/version": http: server gave HTTP response to HTTPS client

## http to an HTTPS controller
error: The controller expects HTTPS. Use an https:// address in --server
cause: 400 Bad Request: Client sent an HTTP request to an HTTPS server.
